 1 big thing — Scoop: Tens of thousands of AI lapses 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 OpenAI, Anthropic and security researchers are investigating tens of thousands of incidents in which frontier models took steps that outside evaluators would consider problematic, Axios' Madison Mills reports. 

 The volume of probes, which occurred in recent months in internal testing and the real world, shows AI's rogue-agent problem is orders of magnitude more complex than is publicly known. Why it matters: The incidents, emerging from internal model assessments and investigations at both companies, raise questions about whether the modelmakers can fully control their technology.

 "What we have seen in terms of what these agents are up to is just the tip of the iceberg," researcher Conrad Stosz at Transluce, an independent AI evaluator, told Axios. 👀 Zoom in : In the past several days, OpenAI and outside researchers have disclosed a litany of episodes of agents going rogue.

 Agents leaked 53 images from ChatGPT users, breached an Australian Medicare portal and attempted to hack other sites, according to the company, sources and reports from Reuters and The New York Times . OpenAI is pausing training on its most capable models and will resume training them "only when we are confident that we have additional safeguards and alignment improvements in place," a spokesperson told Axios. Anthropic has commissioned a third-party safety organization to examine the behavior of its models. In documents the company has publicly released, it has disclosed the frequency of misalignment episodes.

 The system card for its Opus 5.5 model, released this past week, showed how often it behaved in ways the company flagged as unusual or problematic. 💡 Reality check: Some "misaligned behavior," as AI safety pros call it, is to be expected. Bringing the risk of misalignment to zero may not be feasible. 

